.TH DANBYLABTEST 1
.SH NAME
danbylabtest \- check every Danby model written as a danbylab(2) script
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danbylabtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Danbylabtest
checks every
.B *.spec
under
.BR /appl/danby ,
which is every model: a model is a text file in the
.IR dynsys (2)/ danbylab (2)
languages and this reads it whole.
.PP
It used to scrape a
.B SPEC
string constant out of a Limbo module and unescape it by hand, because
that is where a model lived.  That was always a hedge against the string
under test drifting from the string that shipped, and the hedge leaked:
a
.B \eu0027
in a spec meant one thing to the compiler and another to the scraper, so
the two were briefly not the same text at all.  A file cannot drift from
itself, and the scraper is gone.
.PP
Nothing here opens a window.
.B Danbylab->parse
is deliberately the half of the library that needs no display, so this
runs under
.B emu-g
where there is no
.IR draw (2)
and no
.IR tk (2)
at all.  That is the check worth having for the equations: starting a
demo proves it starts.
.PP
Every model is checked for the same four things \(em that the spec
parses, that it declares parameters, that each parameter's default lies
inside its own range, and that no rate is a NaN at the start.  Then it
gets its family's:
.TP
.B mechanism
At least one
.BR link ,
and no point that is not a number at the start.
.TP
.B population
The state count its curriculum page describes, and for several models a
conservation law: a head count that cannot change, an exchange that only
moves goods, a fishery at rest at its bionomic equilibrium.
.TP
.B trajectory
A position's rate is exactly its own velocity, at the start and again
after two hundred steps of the model's own integration; and for the
models with an air term, that zeroing drag, lift and spin leaves plain
free fall.
.TP
.B orbit
Four states per body, a position rate that is its own velocity, and the
one that matters: gravity between bodies is an internal force, so summing
mass times acceleration over every body must give zero.  It does for the
models with an external term too \(em in those the body being pushed has
zero mass and the body being held fixed has zero acceleration \(em so the
one statement covers the whole family.  A model with six bodies has
fifteen pairs and thirty reaction terms, and a sign error or a missing
reaction in any one of them breaks this sum.  It is checked at the
start and again after four hundred steps, because a term that is zero
at the model's own starting configuration and wrong once the bodies
have moved would otherwise pass.
.TP
.B ecology
The equilibrium the spec declares is one: both rates vanish there.
.TP
.B flight
The kinematic identity at twelve states, eleven of them pseudo-random
rather than the model's own; and free fall with every parameter zeroed
but the launch and gravity.
.TP
.B profile
Twelve marches at pseudo-random parameter settings, each checked for
NaNs, for a radius that only increases, for curves that never go
negative, for an enclosed mass that never falls, and for a normalised
curve that reaches exactly one at the outer edge.
.PP
A family with no models left would make its checks dead code with
nothing to say so, so an empty family is itself a failure.
.PP
With
.BR -v ,
each model's family, parameter count and state count are printed as it
is checked.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/danbylabtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danbytest (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR dynsys (2)
.SH BUGS
The
.BR mechanism ,
.B population
and
.B ecology
checks are made at the model's own starting configuration only, so a
term that is zero there and wrong later would pass.  The other four
families do better:
.B trajectory
and
.B orbit
integrate and check again,
.B flight
samples the state space, and
.B profile
samples the parameter space.
.PP
The integration this test does is its own, using
.IR numerics (2)
directly, so that what it checks is the model's equations and not a
second copy of the library's stepper.  Nothing here exercises that
stepper, or the buttons, or a click in a plot.
.PP
Nothing here draws anything, so a model whose equations are right and
whose picture is wrong passes.
